Z||000011||olympic||09-16-2000||11:15 PM||||  I love metric.  It's so much easier to do math with.  The basis for metric is the meter,the liter and the gram. And all measurements are made with powers of ten of these units.  Some common prefixes are<P>micro =1 millionth (0.000001)<BR>milli =1 thousandth (0.001)<BR>centi = 1 hundredth (0.01)<BR>deci  =1 tenth(0.1)<BR>deca  =10<BR>hecta =100<BR>kilo  =1000<BR>mega  =1 000 000<P>So a "kilo"gram is 1000 grams, a "milli"liter is 0.001 litrers and a "centi"meter is 0.01 meters.  All powers of 10.<P>The meter, liter and gram are also related through the mass and volume of pure water.  1 milliliter of water will weigh 1 gram and and occupy 1 cubic centimeter of space.   Clear as mud isn't it?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>   If you talk to anyone my age(27) or older here they will still talk about pounds and miles etc.  I think Canada adopted metric in ~ 1977.  One good thing is we gained 2 mph in the speed limit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  It went from 60 mph to 100 km/h which is 62 mph.<P>  Anyways, someone correct me if I'm wrong but I thought that the USA was the only country left in the world that's not using metric.  ||||reg||10
Z||000012||Monkeyman||09-17-2000||12:43 AM||||Yup.  We're the only ones left to use the English system and darn proud of it.  We don't go fer none of that crazy foreign stuff.  Seriously, I would be lost without my beloved English system of inches, pounds, and  miles, but I think we should change over to metric.  A couple of states tried to on their mile markers, but it didn't work so well.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cheever2||09-17-2000||01:06 AM||||Doesn't Canada use the same clock we do in the US (based on *gasp* twelve)?!  Yeah, and the calendar, again based on *guess what* twelve?!<P>||||reg||4
Z||000014||Cliff Pennock||09-17-2000||02:18 AM||||The metric system is indeed very easy to work with. One liter water weighs exactly one kilogram and its volume is exactly a cube with sides of 10 centimeter. Temperatures are also based on water; The freezing point of water is 0 (zero) degrees Celcius, the boiling point is 100 degrees. I use this to calibrate temperature sensors. Fill a cup with icecubes, add water, mix thoroughly and the temperature of the mixture is exactly 0C. Boil some water and its temperature is exactly 100C.<P>Here are some more interesting metric units:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/metric.gif"> <BR>||||reg||1
